<!-- Saint Matthias
Matthias (Μαθθίας): The Apostle Chosen by Lot

(Parallel to "Simon bar Jonah: A Galilean Fisherman" — though structurally unlike any other entry in this series)

Name, Identity, and Social Position
Greek name "Matthias" (Μαθθίας), a shortened form of "Mattathias," itself related to the Hebrew "Mattityahu" ("gift of Yahweh") — the same underlying root as "Matthew," though the two names and the two men are distinct, worth a brief disambiguating note given how visually similar the names appear on the page
Unlike every other figure in this series, Matthias was not among the Twelve during Jesus's earthly ministry — his entire apostolic identity begins after the resurrection and ascension, making his page's opening section necessarily different in kind from Peter's, Andrew's, or even the sparsely-documented James son of Alphaeus's
Identified in Acts only at the moment of his selection to replace Judas Iscariot (Acts 1:21-26) — no calling narrative, no Gospel appearance, and no prior individual mention exists anywhere in Scripture before this point
Later tradition (Eusebius, citing Clement of Alexandria) numbers him among the wider group of seventy(-two) disciples Jesus sent out during his ministry (Luke 10:1-12) — a plausible but not certain identification, since the Seventy are never named individually in the Gospels
Family Structure & Responsibilities
No family details preserved in Scripture whatsoever — no father's name, no hometown, no trade
This is a more total silence than even James son of Alphaeus, since James at least has a named father (Alphaeus); Matthias has no biographical anchor of any kind prior to Acts 1
Social and Cultural Environment
Acts 1:21-22 establishes the only "biographical" qualification given for his selection: he must be one of the men "who have accompanied us during all the time that the Lord Jesus went in and out among us, beginning from the baptism of John until the day when he was taken up from us"
This qualification implies sustained, long-term discipleship from the very beginning of Jesus's public ministry, even though no specific episode involving him is ever narrated — an unusual case where Scripture tells us he was continuously present without ever telling us what he did or said
Religious Life
His candidacy itself reveals a religious life defined by quiet, sustained faithfulness rather than any recorded dramatic episode — present, by implication, at the baptism, the miracles, the teachings, the crucifixion, and the resurrection appearances, yet never once individually named until Acts 1
Worth framing this explicitly as a distinct category from James son of Alphaeus or Simon the Zealot: those two are silent members of the Twelve; Matthias is a silent witness before the Twelve, whose faithfulness becomes visible only in retrospect
Political Awareness
No information preserved in Scripture
Reputation in the Community
Sufficiently respected among the assembled disciples (a company of about 120, Acts 1:15) to be put forward as one of only two candidates for the vacant apostleship, alongside "Joseph called Barsabbas, who was surnamed Justus" (Acts 1:23)
This nomination itself is the only direct evidence of his standing — being considered worthy of the apostolic office by the gathered believers, prior to any casting of lots
Matthias the Called

(Parallel to "The Day the Nets Were Full" — though here the calling comes through the community and the lot, not directly from Jesus's voice)

Peter initiates the process, citing Psalm 69:25 and Psalm 109:8 as scriptural warrant for filling the vacancy Judas's death and betrayal had created: "Let another take his office" (Acts 1:20)
Two candidates are put forward by the assembled believers: Joseph called Barsabbas (Justus) and Matthias (Acts 1:23) — the only other named figure in this specific narrative, worth a brief note since Barsabbas never appears again in Scripture and represents a genuine "path not taken" in apostolic history
The gathered believers pray before casting lots: "Lord, who knows the hearts of all, show which one of these two you have chosen to take the place in this ministry and apostleship from which Judas turned aside" (Acts 1:24-25)
The lot falls to Matthias, "and he was numbered with the eleven apostles" (Acts 1:26)
This is the only apostolic selection method in the New Testament that uses casting lots rather than Jesus's direct call — a structural singularity worth stating plainly, since every other apostle in this series was called personally by Jesus during his earthly ministry, while Matthias is chosen by a community process after Jesus's ascension
Worth noting a point of ongoing scholarly and denominational discussion: whether this election represents a Spirit-guided precedent for church governance, or whether it was in some sense premature, given that Paul's later, direct calling by the risen Christ (Acts 9; Galatians 1:1) is sometimes read by some interpreters as God's actual intended twelfth apostle — this outline should present both readings evenhandedly rather than favoring one
Matthias's Journey

(Parallel to "Cephas's Journey" — though here almost entirely absent, even more so than James son of Alphaeus or Simon the Zealot)

A Complete Absence of Individual Narrative
No episode, question, statement, or action is attributed to Matthias individually anywhere in Scripture, before or after his selection
Unlike James son of Alphaeus or Simon the Zealot, who at least appear by name multiple times across the four apostolic lists, Matthias appears in exactly one passage in the entire New Testament (Acts 1:15-26) — the smallest scriptural footprint of any apostle in this series
He is not mentioned again anywhere in Acts after his selection, including at Pentecost, where "the Twelve" are presumed to include him but he is not individually named (Acts 2:14 names only Peter "standing with the eleven")
Matthias the Apostle of the Early Church

(Parallel to Peter's leadership: Pentecost, Miracles, Imprisonment, Council)

Holy Spirit Praxis
Presumed present at Pentecost as one of "the Twelve" now that the number had been restored, though — unlike even the quietest apostles in this series — he is not textually confirmed present the way Acts 1:13's list confirms Philip, Bartholomew, Thomas, Matthew, James son of Alphaeus, and Simon
This is a meaningful distinction worth stating directly: those apostles are silent in Acts despite being named in its opening list; Matthias is silent in Acts and was never named in that list at all, since his selection occurs after it
Absence from Individual Narrative in Acts
Matthias never appears again anywhere in the book of Acts following Acts 1:26, making him the most completely unattested apostle in Scripture after his introduction — an even more absolute silence than the pattern already established across Philip, Bartholomew, Thomas, Matthew, James son of Alphaeus, and Simon
The Council of Jerusalem
No individual role attributed to him in Acts 15
Presumed inclusion among "the apostles and elders," though this is now an assumption built on an assumption, since his ongoing standing as one of the Twelve is never explicitly reaffirmed anywhere in the text after Acts 1
Matthias's Later Ministry

(A distinctive tradition-dependent section, though even thinner than Simon the Zealot's or James son of Alphaeus's)

Early Christian tradition is unusually sparse and inconsistent even by the standards already established in this series
Various traditions (transmitted through Clement of Alexandria and later, less reliable sources) place his later ministry in Judea, Cappadocia, on the shores of the Caspian Sea, or in Ethiopia — a spread of candidate locations as wide as Matthew's, but resting on considerably weaker early attestation
Some later legendary traditions describe him ministering among cannibalistic peoples in a distant land (echoing motifs found in the apocryphal Acts of Andrew and Matthias, which some scholars believe originally concerned Matthias before later merging with Andrew's legend) — this outline should flag this material explicitly as legendary and largely unreliable, more so than any tradition attached to Thomas, Bartholomew, or Jude
As with all later-ministry sections in this series, this material should be presented as tradition of the lowest relative reliability yet encountered, given how little even the earliest church historians had to work with
Matthias's Writings

(Parallel to "James son of Alphaeus's Writings" / "Simon the Zealot's Writings")

No canonical writing is attributed to Matthias
A "Gospel of Matthias" is referenced by early church writers (Origen, Eusebius) as circulating among certain groups, but no copy survives, and what little is known of its content (via secondhand quotation, particularly by Clement of Alexandria) suggests Gnostic-influenced material foreign to apostolic teaching, in keeping with the pattern already seen in the apocryphal Gospel of Thomas and Gospel of Philip
This places him alongside James son of Alphaeus and Simon the Zealot as an apostle with no reliable literary legacy of his own — the third member of this "no writings" cluster in the series
The Final Journey

(Parallel to the Final Journey sections of all preceding apostles)

What the Bible Tells Us
Scripture gives no account of his life, ministry, or death beyond the single episode of his selection in Acts 1:15-26 — the most minimal biblical foundation of any figure in this entire series
Matthias's Ministry and Death (Early Christian Tradition)
Tradition is genuinely unsettled on both location and manner of death, arguably even more so than Matthew's divided accounts
Some traditions hold he was stoned and then beheaded in Jerusalem by the Jewish authorities; others place his martyrdom in Colchis (on the eastern shore of the Black Sea, in modern Georgia); still others describe a natural death
This outline should state plainly, as with James son of Alphaeus, that no single account carries meaningfully greater historical weight than the others
Matthias's Martyrdom
No widely dominant tradition exists; the stoning-and-beheading account in Jerusalem is the version most commonly repeated in later Western tradition, but should be presented as one option among several rather than settled fact
Matthias's Legacy

(Parallel to "James son of Alphaeus's Legacy" / "Simon the Zealot's Legacy")

Patron saint of alcoholics, tailors, carpenters, and (fittingly, given the manner of his selection) hope in difficult decisions and those seeking guidance in uncertainty
His enduring theological significance lies almost entirely in the manner of his selection rather than in any personal deed, writing, or narrative: he stands as Scripture's clearest example of communal discernment, prayer, and reliance on God's providence in filling a position of spiritual authority
Frequently invoked in later Christian tradition (and in some traditions of church governance) as a precedent for prayerful discernment in selecting leaders, whether or not the lot-casting method itself is considered normative for the church going forward
Represents, alongside James son of Alphaeus and Simon the Zealot, a third member of the "narrative silence" cluster in this series — but occupies a distinct category within it, since his silence stems from entering the story after its central events rather than from being present throughout without comment
A fitting closing note for the Twelve as a set: Matthias completes the apostolic number not through dramatic encounter or miraculous calling, but through the ordinary, faithful, long-obscured presence of someone who had simply "accompanied" Jesus the whole time — a final, quiet counterpoint to Peter's dramatic call, Paul's blinding vision, and Matthew's abandoned tax booth
Feast days observed separately in Eastern and Western traditions, with some historical variation in date reflecting the same uncertainty that marks the rest of his page -->